ady and what it says, whether Shopify is connected, whether a receipt page is set up, how many variations the catalogue holds and when it was last imported, and the last five notices about syncing, the queue, stock, the reader and general information. Refund and pass notices are left out because they name customers and staff. It does not read any sale, any card attempt, or any notice's body. Every role can use it, because Support & diagnostics is open to every role.\n\n### What it can see about the drawer and the cart\nThree tools read the cash drawer and the sale in progress from a snapshot taken when you ask (`takeCashState` in `src/lib/assistantCashIO.ts`). **Cash drawer** gives the shift, what it should hold, the ledger's notes and coins, today's takings and the last five movements, without staff names or notes. **Cash calculator** does change, split payments and a float suggestion, using `planChange`, `equalPlan`, `firstPlan` and `recommendFloat`. **Current cart** gives the priced lines, totals, price checks and the warnings set to show at Charge. A warning set to queue is only counted, because it goes on the warning list and never stops Charge. Every role can use all three, because the drawer and the cart are open to every role. On practice data the snapshot is empty and the tools say they could not read the till.\n\n### Practice mode\nThe chat is switched off in practice mode, and that includes the search list. Practice mode never sends shop data out. In a training **Check**, the practice bar has an **Ask coach** button beside **Hint**. It is the assistant, but as a coach: tap it, type what you are stuck on, and it answers in a sentence or two without doing the task for you. Each question uses a hint, the same as the **Hint** button, so the bar shows the same hint and the count of hints you used goes up. The first question opens hint 1, the next hint 2, and so on. Asking it to *just tell me* does not skip ahead, because the app decides which hint is open, not the coach. When every written hint has been used, the coach says so and sends you to **Hint**, which is the only thing that highlights the answer. If you have gone off course, it points you at the card in the bar and uses no hint. If the model cannot answer, you still get the hint itself. The coach knows the course name, the goal and the hints opened so far, and nothing else: no shop data, no drawer, no cart, no settings. It can look at help pages and screens for how a feature works in general. Questions to the coach are logged for the manager, marked as practice, under your own name and not the practice cashier's. **Start again** clears the conversation and puts the hints back to the start.",
     "advanced": "### What goes to the model\nOnly the short rules the app gives it, then as many of the newest messages as fit in the **Context size** from the AI assistant settings (a quarter is kept free for the reply). Older messages stay on the iPad for you to scroll, but are not sent. Replies have em and en dashes replaced before they are shown.\n\n### How the buttons work\nThe model asks for a button with the `show_link` tool, giving a kind (help page or screen) and an id it got from a search. The app checks the id against what your role may see, the same check the searches use, so a page or screen you cannot use gets the same answer as one that does not exist. The button is not part of the reply text: it is saved next to the reply, at most three, and it is checked again each time the chat is drawn, so if your role loses access later, the button is gone. Buttons are never sent back to the model. A button can only open a help page, a tab or a page that needs nothing else; it never opens an address on the web and never changes anything. Code: `resolveLink` and `liveLinks` in `src/lib/assistantTools.ts`, the `target` of each screen in `src/lib/assistantScreens.ts`.\n\n### How the guide works\nThe model asks for the button with the `show_guide` tool, giving a screen id from `search_screens` (which marks the screens that can be guided with `can_guide`). The app checks it for your role exactly as it does for `show_link`, so a screen you cannot reach gets the same answer as one that does not exist. Each screen carries its walk: the controls to tap from the Checkout tab, read from the same graph that gives the plain words, with the words said at each tap. A screen gets no walk if any tap has no control or no words, if the control is not a real spot in the till, or if the walk goes into a window. The ring does not remember where you are: several times a second it looks at which controls of the walk are on screen and points at the last one, so tapping something else or starting part of the way along both work. A guide to a tab or a page ends when you arrive there. A guide to a view of Checkout (such as Keypad) ends when one of that view's own controls is on screen, or when you tap the last control. The ring and card are drawn on the main screen only, never inside a window. The guide changes nothing: it only points. Code: `src/lib/assistantGuide.ts` (the rules and the one active guide, tested), `src/ui/AssistantGuide.tsx` (the ring and card), `walk` of each screen in `src/lib/assistantScreens.ts`, `resolveLink` and `show_guide` in `src/lib/assistantTools.ts`.\n\n### How the log is kept\nOne file for each day, in a folder inside the app that Files.app cannot see (`Library/Application Support/assistant-log/`, next to Documents and not inside it). A finished month is packed into one compressed file. The log has a size cap, 200 MB for now. When it is full the oldest files are removed to make room, a *purge* line is written saying how many events went and through which day, and sending is never blocked. Each line records who asked (name and role as they were then), the model and whether the address counted as local or remote, the scrubbed question, the reply, the messages sent (by id once the log already holds them, to keep it small), a checksum of the exact request, and how many items the scrubber hid in each category. The system prompt is kept once in `meta.json`. Code: `src/lib/assistantLog.ts` (logic, tested) and `src/lib/assistantLogIO.ts` (the folder). A failed request is logged too, as an error with the question that was about to be sent.\n\n### The offline list\nSince 0164 the list also shows **products** (price, stock, and a line for each variation) and a **low, out of stock or oversold** list when your question asks for one, for example *what is low on stock* or *red highland cows*. These use the same lookups as the model's product tools, from the catalogue on this iPad, so they work with no connection at all. The words of a question that are not part of a product name (*how many*, *do we have*, *price*, *stock*) are left out of the search; with one or two words all of them must match, and with three or more half is enough. Only active and unlisted products are listed, and nothing is shown in practice mode. The list is not stored. Only your question is, with a marker where a reply would be. Each time the chat is shown the list is searched again, for whoever is signed in then, so someone whose role has less access is never shown something they cannot use, even in an old chat. The model is never shown a question that only got a list, so it does not try to answer it later. A manager sees these in the conversation log as an *Offline search*, with the screens, help pages and products it found and why the model was not used.\n\n### How the product and stock tools work\nThey are in `src/lib/assistantProducts.ts` and use the till's own rules, so the assistant cannot say *low* or *out* differently from the screen: `stockText` from `src/lib/lookup.ts` for the words, and `filterVariants`, `matchesStock` and `sortVariants` from `src/lib/inventoryView.ts` for the filters. `dealsFor` from `lookup.ts` gives the deals that could apply. A search needs every word to match the name, variation, SKU or barcode, as the Inventory search box does. When only some variations of a product match, only those are shown and there is no total for the product, so a total never covers more than it says. Product, price, stock, SKU, barcode and deal fields are *shop data* in the gate; the cost price is *costs and profit*, so it goes out only where that is allowed and is never in the summary form. Not tracked is stock `null` in the catalogue, and stock can be negative (oversold) by design.\n\n### How the shop data tools work\nEach tool is a plain function over a snapshot taken when you ask the question (`takeAssistantData` in `src/lib/assistantDataIO.ts`): this till's sales (the latest 2000) plus the sales and daily totals the other tills have shared, the daily totals saved here (this till's, any sales in the outbox not yet counted, and imported Square history), the customers, the deals and the catalogue names. They are handed nothing that can write, and a test freezes the snapshot to prove they do not change it. Money is returned as text such as $12.50 so a small model never reads cents. Each tool declares a field map that puts every field it can return into a privacy category, and the data gate removes or masks what the endpoint may not receive. A tool also never matches on something the model may not read: a customer is not found by name when names are hidden, and a gift card is not found by code when codes are hidden, because a match would tell the model who bought what. When names and emails are both hidden, a customer search returns an empty list and the privacy note, exactly as it does for a customer who does not exist. How an order was paid is treated as a payment detail and is never sent. Card details are never put in a result at all. On practice data the snapshot is empty, which is a second lock behind the practice mode block. Code: `src/lib/assistantData.ts` (the queries, tested), `src/lib/assistantDataTools.ts` (the tool definitions and field maps), `src/lib/assistantDataIO.ts` (the snapshot).\n\n### How the screen context works\nThe chat screen reads the navigation and your display mode once, at the moment you send a question (`registerContext` in `src/lib/assistantContext.ts`), and the `screen_context` tool hands that snapshot back. The tool never reads the app itself and has nothing that can write. The last pages on the navigation stack are Help and the chat, so they are skipped to find the page you were on (`standingOn`), and the app graph names it (`screenOfRoute` in `src/lib/appGraph.ts`, the same route table training uses). A page the graph does not know is reported as one it cannot name, and the tab under it is not used in its place, because that would say the wrong screen. The page is looked up among the screens your role may reach, so a page your role cannot reach gets exactly the answer a page it cannot name gets. No ids are returned. Every field is *shop data* in the gate and none is personal, so nothing is hidden on any address. Windows are not reported: the list of open windows is only filled in during practice mode, where the chat is switched off. The guides still always start from the Checkout tab. The tool tells the model where you are, but the walks are not yet planned from your current screen.\n\n### How the status tool works\n`app_status` is in `src/lib/assistantStatus.ts`. `takeTillState` (`src/lib/assistantStatusIO.ts`) reads the store once when a question is sent, and `buildStatusView` turns it into a short list of plain values: the app version and build from `react-native-device-info`, `sync` and `zeller` from the store, the outbox (count, blocked items, oldest, distinct errors), the notices of kind sync, queue, stock, reader and info, and the catalogue times. The tool only returns that view, so it cannot read or write anything else. `safeLine` makes each message one short line and replaces web addresses with `[address]`, and the data gate then scrubs emails, phone numbers, card numbers and keys. Every field is *shop data* in the gate. The reader's ready state and message count as the app's own state and not as terminal details; no terminal id, card attempt, amount or reference is ever in the view. On practice data `takeTillState` returns nothing and the tool says it could not read the till. Tests: `tests/assistantstatus.test.ts`.\n\n### How the coach works\nThe logic is in `src/lib/assistantCoach.ts` and the window is `src/ui/CoachSheet.tsx`, opened from `src/ui/TrainingDock.tsx` in a Check only. `planCoachAsk` decides what an ask does from the shared hint state (`src/lib/trainingHints.ts`): raise it one rung and ask the model, or answer by itself when the person is off course or every written hint is used. The coach never raises the ladder to the reveal. `buildCoachPrompt` is built from the course title and summary, the goal's title and the rungs opened so far. It is never given the goal's `reveal`, `until`, `inject`, `watch` or `taughtBy`, so it cannot give away what it does not hold, and a test checks this for every course. The model has three tools (`search_docs`, `read_doc`, `search_screens`) and at most 4 calls a question. After the model answers, `coachLeaks` compares every number in the reply with the numbers in the goal, the opened hints and the question. A reply with a number from nowhere is thrown out and the hint itself is shown. Turns made above the rung the person is on are not sent to the model and are dropped from the thread when **Start again** puts the ladder back. The thread is kept in memory only. Each question is written to the audit log with `practice: true` through `coachLogSink` in `src/lib/assistantLogIO.ts`, using `realSignIn` in `src/state/trainingSession.ts` for the real staff member and role.\n\n### Limits of this version\nThe offline list looks up products and stock, but not customers or sales yet. The tools are for the help pages, the screens, the button and guide tools, the seven shop data tools above, the date and time, where you were, how the till is doing, and the cash drawer, cash sums and cart. There is no live look at Shopify, so a gift card balance is out of reach; other tills' sales are here only as far as those tills have shared them. A request is written to the log after the model answers or fails, so if the app is closed in the middle of a request, that request leaves no line. Phone number hiding covers Australian numbers and numbers that start with a plus sign, so a number typed another way may not be caught. Do not type customer details you would not want sent."
